    {
      "obligation": "refuter:13:1",
      "id": "lem-basic-bounding-and-dominating-relations",
      "location": "1.2",
      "defect": "unlicensed-inference",
      "evidence": "Step 1.2 says “d is a limit ordinal because d≥ℵ1 will follow from steps 1.1 and 1.5”; step 1.5 sets λ=cf(d) and relies on [F2] for a strictly increasing cofinal map into d, while [F2] only states that such a map exists for a limit ordinal. Thus step 1.5 uses the limit fact that step 1.2 defers to it. A preliminary argument ruling out finite dominating families would establish that d is infinite and close this brief gap.",
      "severity": "nonfatal",
      "observed_sha256": "245506443038a1960d2e3970e88aec383ca3d79159a5ac9cdfa5f4cc6b1d32d1"
    },
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:13:2",
      "id": "ex-countable-decreasing-family-has-a-pseudointersection",
      "location": "4.1",
      "defect": "unlicensed-inference",
      "evidence": "Step 4.1 concludes that the sequence is “strictly increasing” solely because “by step 3.1 the x_n are pairwise distinct.” Pairwise distinctness does not imply increasing. The nested intersections B_n⊆B_{n−1} and the minimum choices would establish monotonicity, but that argument is unstated; distinctness alone already suffices for X to be infinite.",
      "severity": "nonfatal",
      "observed_sha256": "4ca816c913562fcd64c1667ff11e588a988cbe69aee31024c6cfaa225e3a0da1"
